realloc says WHICH KIND of wrong pointer it was handed.

`realloc: pointer was never returned by malloc` is true and useless. It cannot
distinguish three situations that need opposite investigations:

  * the block was FREED earlier -- a use-after-free, and its node is sitting on
    _free_list where the walk never looks
  * the pointer is INTERIOR to a live block -- an offset was added somewhere,
    or a header was skipped
  * the address is not related to any block at all -- it was computed wrongly

This checks all three before dying, and when none of them fits it reports the
distance to the NEAREST block start in either list. That last number is the one
that pays: a pointer sitting a fixed small distance from a block is a header
skip -- tcc's own tal_realloc_impl computes `((tal_header_t *)p) - 1` -- while a
large distance means the address is unrelated to the heap and the fault is
upstream of the allocator.

MEASURED, on mc-tcc compiling tests2/00_assignment.c against tcc-test-shim:

    realloc: pointer was never returned by malloc (5733536)
        -> not on the free list        so NOT a use-after-free
        -> not interior to any block   so NOT a header skip or an offset
        -> nearest block start is 288 bytes away

288 IS NOT A NEW NUMBER IN THIS SPIKE. MICRO-C.md's earlier frontier -- the
two-slot write into table_ident, recorded as closed -- reported its two
overwritten values as `288 bytes apart`. Whether that is the same structure is
not established here, and it should not be assumed; it is written down because
the coincidence is worth one grep by whoever picks this up, and because
"already closed" was the reason nobody looked again.

WHY THE ALLOCATOR ITSELF IS NOT THE SUSPECT. main-06-realloc.c passes, and
main-07-realloc-sequences.c -- added alongside this -- exercises seven shapes
main-06 does not: a block reused from the free list, chained reallocs, a grow
with another block freed in between, a shrink, sixty-four allocations of churn
before growing the oldest block, twenty-four reallocs interleaved with mallocs,
and content survival across a move. All seven pass. The allocator handles
everything asked of it; something upstream is producing an address that was
never a block.

Costs one list walk on a path that ends in exit().

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SECOND ROUND: the diagnostic answered, and the answer was not what it looked
like.

The three-way split above says the pointer is not on the free list and not
interior to a live block. Extended here to print the pointer, the nearest
block, the signed distance, that block's size, the requested size, the whole
allocated and free lists, and -- the one that settled it -- THE MEMORY AT THE
POINTER AND THE FOUR WORDS BELOW IT.

Measured, mc-tcc compiling tests2/00_assignment.c with tcc-test-shim:

    realloc: bad ptr        = 5779744
    realloc: nearest block  = 5779456      +288, one allocation stride
    realloc: nearest size   = 256          so ptr is 32 bytes past its end
    realloc: live blocks    = 15
    realloc: ptr[-4] (next?)= 6927552
    realloc: ptr[-3] (blk?) = 5779744      <-- EQUALS THE BAD POINTER
    realloc: ptr[-2] (size?)= 256
    realloc: ptr[-1] (used?)= 1            <-- _IN_USE

The 32 bytes below the pointer are a `struct _malloc_node` whose `block` field
is exactly that pointer, whose size is 256 and whose used flag says IN USE.

THE BLOCK IS ALLOCATED. ITS NODE IS INTACT. THE LIST DOES NOT REACH IT.

So `realloc: pointer was never returned by malloc` was true of the LIST and
false of the HEAP, and every reading of it as an allocator bug or a caller bug
was chasing a symptom.

RULED OUT, each by measurement:

  * the allocator's logic -- main-06 and main-07 pass, the latter over seven
    reuse shapes written for this
  * use-after-free -- the free-list walk here does not fire, AND disabling
    free() entirely (nothing ever leaves the allocated list) reproduces the
    failure unchanged
  * an interior pointer or a tal header skip -- the range walk here does not
    fire
  * the malloc/realloc signature mismatch between M2libc and micro-c-libc --
    real, worth tidying, and not this
  * duplicate symbols -- _allocated_list, _free_list, malloc, free, realloc and
    _malloc_insert_block are each defined exactly once in the link

WHAT IS LEFT is that the allocated list is losing nodes: a `next` pointer
somewhere in the chain has been clobbered, so the walk stops early and every
node beyond it is invisible while its block stays live. That is heap
corruption by a store with a wrongly-computed address, upstream of the
allocator entirely -- the same shape as the earlier table_ident report, and
288 keeps appearing because it is the allocator's stride for a minimum block
(32-byte node + 256-byte block), not because it is a structure size.

NEXT, AND IT NEEDS NO WATCHPOINT: keep a monotonic count of nodes inserted and
compare it to the reachable count at every allocator entry. The first
divergence brackets the corruption between two allocator calls, which is what
the marker trail can then name. Watchpoints are unavailable under qemu-user --
gdbstub answers Z0/Z1 and refuses Z2/Z3/Z4 -- so this is the available route.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THIRD ROUND: a node-loss detector, and what it caught.

The diagnostics above identify the pointer as a live block whose node the list
does not reach. This adds the check that catches that AT THE MOMENT IT
HAPPENS: a node is created exactly once, in _malloc_add_new, and afterwards
only MOVES between the two lists -- so reachable(allocated) + reachable(free)
must always equal _malloc_nodes_created. _malloc_check_lists() compares them at
every malloc and free, and both walks are bounded so a chain clobbered into a
CYCLE reports rather than hangs.

It fires, and it brackets the damage to a single allocator interval:

    MALLOC CHECK: NODES LOST at malloc
      nodes created    = 79
      nodes reachable  = 17
      lost             = 62
      allocated depth  = 13
      last node addr   = 6877408
      its block        = 6877440
      its size         = 512
      its next (BAD)   = 0        <-- clobbered to NULL

62 nodes cut off in one store, so the break is near the HEAD of the chain.

`next` is at offset 0 of the node, which is 32 bytes before its block -- and
that is exactly where the PREVIOUS allocation's data ends. So this is an
OVERRUN of the neighbouring block, not a stray write at a random address. The
patch now names that neighbour:

      OVERRUN SOURCE blk = 6877152
       its size          = 256
       word[0]           = 16
       word[1]           = 0x011e780400527a01

word[1] is `01 7a 52 00 04 78 1e 01` -- version 1, augmentation string "zR",
code alignment 4, data alignment 0x78 (SLEB -8), return-address register 30.
That is a DWARF CIE for aarch64, and the block's tail holds CFA opcodes. THE
OVERRUNNING BLOCK IS tcc's .eh_frame SECTION DATA.

RULED OUT SINCE, both by compiling the reduced logic and diffing against gcc:

  * CString growth -- cstr_cat's `if (size > cstr->size_allocated)` gate, the
    doubling in cstr_realloc, and the 16-byte struct layout. Correct.
  * section_add -- `offset = (sec->data_offset + align - 1) & -align` with a
    4-byte `int` align against 8-byte `unsigned long` offsets, the growth gate
    `offset1 > sec->data_allocated`, and 400 iterations checking that
    allocated never falls behind offset. Correct, including that the mask
    sign-extends and does not clear the top half of a 64-bit offset.

So the section bookkeeping is right and something writes past the end anyway.
The next question is who writes .eh_frame: a pointer from section_ptr_add held
across a later section_realloc would write through a stale base, which is the
classic shape and is not covered by either probe above.

NOTE ON COST. The check walks both lists at every malloc and free. That is
O(n^2) over a run and it is a DEBUGGING patch, not a shipping one -- it is here
because the fault is live, and it should come out once the corruption is fixed.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE PER-CALL DETECTOR IS OFF BY DEFAULT.

_malloc_check_lists walks both lists at every malloc and free. That is O(n^2)
over a run and, worse, it CHANGES THE TIMING AND THE HEAP of the program under
test -- a "regression" was measured against a tree still carrying it armed
before that was noticed, and the wrong change was blamed for it. Set
_malloc_check_armed to 1 by hand when hunting node loss.

The failure-path diagnostics -- the three-way classification, the node
inspection, the list dump, the nearest-block distance -- cost nothing on a
healthy run and stay on.

